This repository contains no Microsoft-owned files. That is a hard constraint, not a
preference: `tools/check_no_microsoft_files.sh` fails the build if a `.spd`, `.sdf`,
`.LXA`, `sapi.dll`, `spttseng.dll` or a rendered `.wav` ever becomes a tracked file.

What is here
------------
serve/            the HTTP server (MIT)
tools/            corpus, comparison and verification tooling (MIT)
third_party/      the speech engine, carried as a git submodule (see below)

The engine
----------
Path:    third_party/ms-sam-mike-mary-decomp
Upstream: https://github.com/KamiKitsune420/ms-sam-mike-mary-decomp
License: MIT
Pinned:  5852cb209407b5beaa9311601be1bb36d1ca72ee

The pin is on the branch `norm-duration-bounds` of a fork, not on upstream: upstream
`cf1122c` plus one change, bounding the number normalizer's search for a duration
separator so it stays inside its token. `tools/fuzz_tts.c` reproduces the read past the
buffer end against `cf1122c`, and is clean against the pin. The corpus renders identically
under both, so the fix moves no audio. When upstream takes it, move the pin back to the
upstream URL and drop the fork.

The engine is a reimplementation of Microsoft's SAPI 5 text-to-speech engine written from
a disassembly and decompilation of `spttseng.dll`, for interoperability. Its code is the
maintainer's, not Microsoft's. It is not "clean room": disassembly was the input, and
claiming otherwise would misstate the situation. Upstream's own README notes that a few
small tables (`src/sam_abbrev_tab.h`, `src/sam_pos_rules.h`, `src/sam_words_tab.h`: abbreviations,
tagger rules, special words) were transcribed from the engine because the program needs them
to behave the same way. Read its README for what it covers.

Because the engine commit determines the samples that come out, a submodule bump is a
user-visible change: `.github/workflows/regression.yml` fails until the baseline is
re-recorded on purpose.

  ./tools/render_port.sh
  (cd out/port && sha256sum *.wav) > tools/port_hashes.txt

Voice data
----------
The `.spd`, `.sdf` and `.LXA` files are Microsoft's, and the server is useless without
them. They are not distributed here and are not fetched by the build. You supply them from
Microsoft's own sources:

  Microsoft Speech SDK 5.1, SpeechSDK51.exe
    https://download.microsoft.com/download/B/4/3/B4314928-7B71-4336-9DE7-6FA4CF00B7B3/SpeechSDK51.exe
    71,278,472 bytes
    sha256 826ba6df0d38d29560250dc697a0cb53cd57817db1b39c575ee0f31ba78386ba

  The installer is self-extracting: `7z x SpeechSDK51.exe` yields `Data.Cab`, and `7z x
  Data.Cab` yields the payload under names like `F366_Sam.SPD.<guid>`. Rename them to
  `<voice>.spd` / `<voice>.sdf` in the data directory: the scanner matches the extension in
  lower case, and the file name is the voice name.

  `sam.spd`, `sam.sdf` and `r1033tts.lxa` are the same files in the SDK and in a Windows XP
  install. `LTTS1033.LXA` is not: the SDK copy is
  `58c8989b064eea3501b1471a9ee565bed1861daa49b4ebbb1de9a6f038097e19` and XP's is
  `1c2ae5888acc337add107bae02e45554ffc4e7750ea7c354dedacd0c412ab7f6`. The port ships the SDK
  lexicon, so a reference corpus for `tools/golden_test.sh` has to use it too.

Your license to Microsoft's speech software is yours to establish. Whether rendering audio
with these voices is permitted for your use is a question this repository does not answer.

